@page "/access-denied"

@* Reached by REDIRECT, not by routing: the cookie handler sends a signed-in-but-denied
   user here because Program.cs set options.AccessDeniedPath = "/access-denied".

   This page must NOT carry [Authorize]. It is the page shown when authorization has
   already said no, so requiring authorization to view it would loop. *@

<PageTitle>Access Denied</PageTitle>

<h1>Access Denied</h1>

<div class="alert alert-danger">
    <p>You are signed in, but your account does not have permission to view that page.</p>
</div>

<AuthorizeView>
    <Authorized>
        <p>
            Signed in as <strong>@context.User.Identity?.Name</strong>.
        </p>
        @* Printing the roles turns "it just said no" into something a student can
           diagnose: compare this list against the [Authorize(Roles = "...")] on the
           page that refused you. An EMPTY list here is the RoleClaimType bug from
           earlier today -- the roles arrived, but the app was not told to read them. *@
        <p>Roles carried by this identity:</p>
        <ul>
            @foreach (var role in context.User.FindAll(context.User.Identities.First().RoleClaimType))
            {
                <li><code>@role.Value</code></li>
            }
        </ul>
    </Authorized>
</AuthorizeView>

<a href="/">Return to the home page</a>
